// _tests_/bit/pollfree/exceptions.bit — the reviewed exception list for the
// poll-free gate (#3001's split of `_tests_/bit/pollfree.bit`, which crossed
// the 800-line limit once #3000 added group (f5)'s three deadline-bounded
// ABI wrappers).
//
// Sibling of `pollfree.bit` in the same directory module: no `import`, one
// shared scope. This file is pure data — no consts, no structs — and calls
// nothing beyond the builtin `append`, so the import-per-module trap
// (#2867's `rootabi` split hit it first: re-importing a name the module
// already imports elsewhere is `E0042 already declared in this scope`) does
// not apply here. `pollFreeExceptions()` below is what `pollfree.bit`'s
// `main` calls; the four `exceptions*()` tables it assembles, and every
// per-entry justification comment, moved here VERBATIM from the original
// file.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The reviewed exception list — carried across VERBATIM from the deleted
// harness `rootabi_pollfree`, per-entry justifications intact. It was
// hand-audited and cannot be cheaply reconstructed.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Functions under the covered subtrees that are deliberately NOT poll-free.
//
// EVERY ENTRY IS A CLAIM THAT THE FUNCTION HOLDS NO GC OBJECT AS A RAW
// ADDRESS, not merely that annotating it was inconvenient. Adding a line here
// is a review decision; the groups and their reasons:
//
// (a) `float{big,fmt,parse}.bit` and the two `floats.bit` wrappers — genuinely
//     MANAGED Bit code, allocating bignum limb arrays, digit buffers and
//     `string`s, so `@nosplit` (which forbids allocation) is not available to
//     them. #1658 gave them the property instead of the annotation: every raw
//     address now lives inside `packLowBytes`/`unpackToWords`, which ARE
//     `@nosplit`, and the outer bodies hold only managed values.
// (b) `rootconfig.bit`/`testindex.bit` — they read the kernel-supplied `envp`
//     block, so every address they carry is outside the heap. They could not be
//     annotated anyway: `len(<literal>)` is not `@nosplit`-callable.
// (c) `{darwin,linux}/boot.bit`'s boot machinery — must reach the scheduler,
//     thread and park layers and run user `main`, which E0075 would forbid. It
//     carries only raw kernel addresses (argv/envp/auxv, `mapPages` stacks).
// (d) `{darwin,linux}/time.bit`'s three ABI pins — every parameter and result
//     is a scalar `int`; no object crosses them in either direction.
// (g) `runtime/net/windows/**` (#3340, #3595) — the IOCP/overlapped-I/O
//     twin of (f). Same claim (b) as `runtime/net`'s park layer, plus a
//     second, INDEPENDENT reason several of these entries need: this
//     provider's engine (`../../sched/windows/poll.bit`) carries no
//     `@nosplit` function at all, so any caller that reaches
//     `pollClaimOp`/`pollAssociate`/`pollRegister`/`pollOp*` cannot be
//     `@nosplit` regardless of whether it allocates. See
//     `exceptionsNetWindows`'s own header for the four sub-groups.
//
// The one list is spelled across four functions ONLY because a single one
// exceeds `max-fn-lines`. The split follows the group boundaries above, so no
// entry is separated from the reasoning that admitted it; `pollFreeExceptions`
// is the whole list and is the only thing the gate reads.
// Appends every element of `more` to `out`, in order. Pulled out of
// pollFreeExceptions so each group there costs one call instead of one
// `for` loop — ten inlined loops pushed that function's own complexity
// over the limit with no branching logic of its own.
fn extendStrings(out: []string, more: []string): []string {
  for e of more {
    out = append(out, e)
  }
  return out
}

fn pollFreeExceptions(): []string {
  let out = []string(0)
  out = extendStrings(out, exceptionsFloatText())
  out = extendStrings(out, exceptionsEnvReaders())
  out = extendStrings(out, exceptionsBootMachinery())
  out = extendStrings(out, exceptionsClocks())
  out = extendStrings(out, exceptionsBootWait())
  out = extendStrings(out, exceptionsRandNetEngine())
  out = extendStrings(out, exceptionsNetAbi())
  out = extendStrings(out, exceptionsNetWindows())
  out = extendStrings(out, exceptionsNetTcpEngine())
  out = extendStrings(out, exceptionsNetUdpEngine())
  return out
}

// (a) managed float text — see `runtime/root/floats.bit`'s SEAM 5a block.
//
// #3067 added `decUnderscoreOk`/`hexUnderscoreOk` to this group: each takes
// the same MANAGED `[]byte` its caller (`parseBits`/`parseHexBits`, already
// listed below) already holds and reads two neighboring bytes from it. No
// raw GC address is ever formed — the slice reference itself is what stays
// live. They cannot be `@nosplit` for a purely mechanical reason: indexing a
// slice lowers to `rt_call slice_get`, and E0075's Index case admits only a
// fixed-array base, never a slice — so the annotation is refused regardless
// of whether the body is actually poll-free. `isDecDigit`, their sibling
// helper, takes a `byte` by value with no indexing and IS `@nosplit`
// (mechanically proven, not reviewed).
fn exceptionsFloatText(): []string {
  return [
    "root:decUnderscoreOk",
    "root:hexUnderscoreOk",
    "root:bigLimbs",
    "root:bigNew",
    "root:bigSetU64",
    "root:bigNorm",
    "root:bigCopy",
    "root:bigCmp",
    "root:bigAdd",
    "root:bigSub",
    "root:bigMulSmall",
    "root:bigMulAddSmall",
    "root:bigShl",
    "root:bigShr1",
    "root:bigBitLen",
    "root:bigIsZero",
    "root:bigMulPow10",
    "root:fmtMaxDigits",
    "root:fmtBits",
    "root:dragon4",
    "root:highTest",
    "root:render",
    "root:zeros",
    "root:u64BitLen",
    "root:floorDiv",
    "root:parseMaxDigits",
    "root:parseBits",
    "root:assembleBits",
    "root:hexDigitVal",
    "root:parseHexBits",
    "root:roundShiftRight",
    "root:infBits",
    "root:rtStringFromFloat",
    "root:rtParseFloat",
  ]
}

// (b) envp readers, (c) boot machinery, (d) clocks and sleep.
// (b) envp readers — no heap object in play.
fn exceptionsEnvReaders(): []string {
  return [
    "root:gcEnvValuePtr",
    // rootWidthsOk (#3874) — compares `gcState`/`heapBlock`'s declared array
    // lengths against the `gcWords`/`heapWords` consts they claim to be sized
    // by. Mechanically cannot be `@nosplit`: `len()` on a fixed-size array is
    // barred from a `@nosplit` body by SPEC §10.3.1 even though it lowers to
    // a compile-time `ConstInt` with no call at all (#3974 tracks lifting
    // that restriction). THE CLAIM: it holds no GC object at all, as a raw
    // address or otherwise — its only inputs are two array lengths and two
    // integer consts, all resolved at compile time, and its only operation
    // is two `==` comparisons. Every caller of `rootInit` (all three
    // platforms' `boot`, already listed in group (c) below) calls this
    // first and refuses to boot on a mismatch.
    "root:rootWidthsOk",
    "root:gcEnvKeyMatch",
    "root:gcCstrEq",
    "root:gcCstrUsize",
    "root:gcEnvEnabled",
    "root:gcEnvStress",
    "root:gcEnvMinTrigger",
    "root:gcEnvGrowthPct",
    "root:gcEnvMarkStack",
    "root:gcEnvWorkers",
    // #4313's explicit-`BIT_WORKERS` detector — same claim as `gcEnvWorkers`
    // right above it: only `gcEnvValuePtr`'s `envp`-block pointer and
    // `gcCstrUsize`'s parsed `int` are in play, no heap object either way.
    "root:gcEnvWorkersExplicit",
    "root:gcEnvVerbose",
    "root:rootConfigureFromEnv",
    "root:rootEnvVerbose",
    "root:rootEnvMarkStack",
    "root:rootEnvWorkers",
    // #4313's pinned wrapper for `gcEnvWorkersExplicit`, same reason as
    // `rootEnvWorkers` immediately above.
    "root:rootEnvWorkersExplicit",
    // #4325's shared `workersCeiling` decision, extracted out of `boot`'s two
    // platform copies (../darwin/boot.bit, ../linux/boottail.bit) to remove
    // the duplicated branch that pushed both past their complexity baseline.
    // Narrower claim than the wrapper above it: its only inputs are a `bool`
    // and an already-clamped `int`, no `envp` pointer at all, so no heap
    // object is ever in play.
    "root:rootWorkersCeiling",
    "root:testIndexParse",
    "root:rtTestIndex",
  ]
}

// (c) boot machinery — reaches the scheduler/thread/park layers and `main`.
fn exceptionsBootMachinery(): []string {
  return [
    "root/darwin:workerBody",
    "root/darwin:joinWorker",
    "root/darwin:mainTrampoline",
    "root/darwin:boot",
    "root/darwin:machoMain",
    // `tryGrowPool` (#3569) calls this platform's `threadStart`, which is not
    // `@nosplit` and genuinely cannot be (it is a real syscall wrapper), so
    // E0075 rules out the `@nosplit` route outright — same shape as
    // `workerBody`/`boot` just above. Category (b): every address it holds
    // across that call — the scheduler block, the worker/arg/done arrays, the
    // armed start block — is module-static or a caller-owned raw region
    // (`schedInit`'s "workers laid out contiguously" pattern), never a
    // GC-managed object, so there is nothing for the collector to move or
    // sweep out from under it even if `threadStart` polled internally.
    // LOCK CLAUSE (#3761): "nothing to sweep" was about references, not
    // locks. `tryGrowPool` holds `scRegLock` (also `stwScanTasks`'s lock)
    // across this call; `trampClaim`'s back-edge poll was reachable under
    // it — a real deadlock, closed by `@nosplit` on `trampClaim`.
    "root/darwin:tryGrowPool",
    "root/linux:workerBody",
    "root/linux:joinWorker",
    "root/linux:mainTrampoline",
    "root/linux:boot",
    "root/linux:initLinuxTls",
    "root/linux:rtStartMain",
    // LOCK CLAUSE (#3812) — the Darwin entry above got this in #3761; this one
    // did not, and the gap is exactly what let the twin bug ship: this
    // `tryGrowPool` also holds `scRegLock` across its `threadStart` call, and
    // Linux's `threadStart` reaches its own back-edge pollers through
    // CALLEES IN OTHER OBJECTS — `../../thread/linux/tls.bit`'s
    // `tlsAreaBytes`/`tlsPrepare`, which walk this image's `PT_TLS` program
    // header via `getauxval`/`tlsPhdr` (`runtime/auxv`) — so a byte-range scan
    // of `bit_rt_port_thread_start` itself (#3761's own Linux check) finds
    // nothing. #3812 closed the window by caching that walk's result once, at
    // boot, before `scRegLock` is ever taken (`initLinuxTls` above primes it),
    // and marking `tlsPrepare` — now geometry-cache-only plus one unavoidable
    // per-thread byte copy — `@nosplit`. Same shape as Darwin's `trampClaim`
    // fix: "nothing to sweep" was about references, not locks.
    "root/linux:tryGrowPool",
    // Windows (#3330, epic #3322) — the same four roles as above (entry
    // point, thread body, trampoline, boot), with no separate `joinWorker`:
    // `boot` joins each worker with `WaitForSingleObject` on its own
    // `CreateThread` HANDLE inline, natively OS-bounded, so there is no
    // manually-polled "done word" to make a fifth function. Verified
    // mechanically, not merely by matching the platforms above: adding
    // `@nosplit` to `boot` is refused by E0075 on `rootEnvMarkStack`,
    // `rootConfigureFromEnv` and `rootEnvWorkers` (none marked nosplit), and
    // to `workerBody` on `schedFindWorkAt` and `schedWorkerStepAt` — both
    // reach the scheduler exactly as their Darwin/Linux twins do. `winMain`
    // cascades from `boot` not being nosplit (E0075 on the `boot()` call
    // itself). `mainTrampoline` mechanically type-checks `@nosplit` (its only
    // calls are the extern `bit_main` and the already-`@nosplit`
    // `schedTaskExit`), but carries the same claim as its twins rather than
    // the annotation: it holds only `mainExitCode`, an `int`, never a GC
    // object as a raw address, across the boot sequence.
    "root/windows:workerBody",
    "root/windows:mainTrampoline",
    "root/windows:boot",
    "root/windows:winMain",
  ]
}

// (d) clocks and sleep — scalars only, and `sleep_ns` parks.
fn exceptionsClocks(): []string {
  return [
    "root/darwin:rtTimeMonoNs",
    "root/darwin:rtTimeUnixNs",
    "root/darwin:rtTimeSleepNs",
    "root/linux:rtTimeMonoNs",
    "root/linux:rtTimeUnixNs",
    "root/linux:rtTimeSleepNs",
    // Windows (#3336) only needs `rtTimeSleepNs` here — `rtTimeMonoNs` and
    // `rtTimeUnixNs` are already `@nosplit` in ../windows/time.bit, so this
    // gate does not flag them and they need no entry.
    "root/windows:rtTimeSleepNs",
  ]
}

// (e) `runtime/rand`, and (f1)/(f2) of `runtime/net`.
// (c2) `bootWaitForMain` — the boot thread's step-7 wait for `main`, split out
// of each platform's `boot` by #3717 when the 1ms poll it used to be became a
// `parkWait` park. This is group (c) unchanged: the same machinery, in the same
// files, reaching the same park layer, and both its parent `boot` and its
// closest sibling `joinWorker` (which calls the very same `parkSleepNs`) are
// already listed above for that reason.
//
// VERIFIED MECHANICALLY rather than by matching its neighbours, as the Windows
// entries above are: adding `@nosplit` is refused by E0075 on `parkWait` and on
// `parkSleepNs`, on all three platforms, and both are real syscall wrappers
// (`__ulock_wait`/`futex`/`WaitOnAddress`, and `nanosleep`) that genuinely
// cannot carry the annotation.
//
// THE CLAIM, which is what an entry here means: it holds no GC object at all,
// as a raw address or otherwise. Its entire state is `ptrOf(mainDone)`, a
// module-level `i32`, and `ptrOf(bootSleepReq)`, a module-level `[2]i64`
// scratch — neither is a managed object, so there is nothing for the collector
// to sweep out from under it. It is also the one function on this list that
// spends its life INSIDE `gcBlockingBegin`/`gcBlockingEnd`, the bracket whose
// own precondition is that the thread holds no live Bit reference at all.
fn exceptionsBootWait(): []string {
  return [
    "root/darwin:bootWaitForMain",
    "root/linux:bootWaitForMain",
    "root/windows:bootWaitForMain",
  ]
}

fn exceptionsRandNetEngine(): []string {
  return [
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // (e) `runtime/rand` (#1667, #3338) — one per platform, and only because
    //     `panic` is not `@nosplit`-callable. The allocate-and-fill half IS
    //     `@nosplit` (`randomBytesFill`); what is left here is straight-line
    //     with NO LOOP, and the one call it makes while `s` is set — `panic`
    //     — is on the branch where `s` is the `randEntropyFailed` sentinel
    //     rather than an object, and never returns. See `rand/*/random.bit`.
    //     `rand/windows` is its own module (#2887's per-module keying), so it
    //     needs its own entry despite carrying an identical argument to its
    //     two siblings.
    "rand/darwin:rtRandomBytes",
    "rand/linux:rtRandomBytes",
    "rand/windows:rtRandomBytes",
    // #3992: `root/windows:winRetireTaskStack`
    // (`runtime/root/windows/bootstack.bit`) — the same root reason as the
    // three entries above (`panic` is not `@nosplit`-callable), a different
    // module: releasing a retired task's stack reservation, not filling a
    // random buffer. Straight-line, no loop, one parameter (`region`, the
    // raw OS address `mapPages` returned when this task's stack was mapped
    // — never a GC-tracked reference), and the one call it makes while
    // `region` is live — `panic` — is on the failure branch and never
    // returns.
    "root/windows:winRetireTaskStack",
    // #4120: `root/windows:winRetireTaskBlock`
    // (`runtime/root/windows/bootstack.bit`) — same module, same reason as
    // the entry directly above: releasing the task control block's own
    // reservation instead of the stack's. Straight-line, no loop, one
    // parameter (`addr`, the raw OS address `mapPages` returned when this
    // task's control block was mapped — never a GC-tracked reference), and
    // the one call it makes while `addr` is live — `panic` — is on the
    // failure branch and never returns.
    "root/windows:winRetireTaskBlock",
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // (f) `runtime/net` (#1668) — THE PARK LAYER. Every entry from here down
    //     rests on claim (b) in this file's header: it holds no raw GC
    //     address, so a poll or a park inside it is harmless. `@nosplit` is not
    //     merely inconvenient for these, it is unavailable — E0075 is transitive
    //     and the subtree reaches `schedPark`. Each was confirmed refused by the
    //     compiler rather than assumed: marking them yields
    //     "calls 'netAwaitReady', which is not marked nosplit" (the engine),
    //     "calls 'fsOpen'" (`readResolvConf`) or "may not allocate" (the four
    //     wrappers that own a managed scratch).
    //
    //     (f1) The socket engine. Buffers arrive as `*u8` PARAMETERS pointing
    //     into memory the caller owns and keeps alive; these frames hold no
    //     object address of their own. `netAwaitReady` is the parker itself.
    "net/darwin:netAwaitReady",
    "net/linux:netAwaitReady",
    "net/darwin:netAcceptTcp",
    "net/darwin:netDialTcp",
    "net/darwin:netReadSock",
    "net/darwin:netWriteSock",
    "net/linux:netAcceptTcp",
    "net/linux:netDialTcp",
    "net/linux:netReadSock",
    "net/linux:netWriteSock",
    "net/darwin:netRecvFrom",
    "net/darwin:netSendTo",
    "net/linux:netRecvFrom",
    "net/linux:netSendTo",
    //     (f2) `readResolvConf` — reads into the caller's managed `[]i64`
    //     scratch through a `*u8`; owns no object. Refused `@nosplit` because
    //     `fsOpen`/`fsClose` are RtFn builtins.
    "net/darwin:readResolvConf",
    "net/linux:readResolvConf",
  ]
}

// (f3) and (f4) of `runtime/net` — the `netabi.bit` ABI wrappers.
fn exceptionsNetAbi(): []string {
  return [
    //     (f3) The ABI wrappers that pass a caller's `string` STRAIGHT THROUGH
    //     to the parking engine as `strData(s)`. `s` is a raw `int` here, but
    //     the object is rooted by the compiled Bit caller's own stack-map slot —
    //     the runtime never allocated it, so unlike #1656's `rtFsReadAll` there
    //     IS a frame that names it, and the precise walk covers every frame of
    //     the running task.
    "net/darwin:netAbiAccept",
    "net/darwin:netAbiDial",
    "net/darwin:netAbiUdpSend",
    "net/darwin:netAbiWrite",
    "net/linux:netAbiAccept",
    "net/linux:netAbiDial",
    "net/linux:netAbiUdpSend",
    "net/linux:netAbiWrite",
    //     (f4) The four wrappers that ALLOCATE a receive buffer — this ticket's
    //     actual defect, and now the reason they cannot be `@nosplit`: their
    //     buffers are MANAGED `[]i64` locals and `@nosplit` forbids allocation
    //     outright. That is the fix, not a workaround; a managed local is a
    //     stack-map slot, which is precisely what the raw `rtStrAlloc` address
    //     they used to hold was not.
    "net/darwin:netAbiRead",
    "net/darwin:netAbiUdpRecv",
    "net/darwin:netAbiResolve",
    "net/linux:netAbiRead",
    "net/linux:netAbiUdpRecv",
    "net/linux:netAbiResolve",
    //     #2552 hoisted `netAbiUdpSenderHost` out of both providers into the
    //     shared `runtime/net/netabi.bit`, so its scope key is `net:`, not the
    //     two provider keys it had before. The (f4) reasoning above is unchanged
    //     — the move did not touch the body.
    "net:netAbiUdpSenderHost",
    //     (f5) #2291/#3000: the three deadline-bounded ABI wrappers that pack a
    //     caller's word-strided `[]byte` into a PACKED scratch before handing it
    //     to the deadline-bounded socket engine below (`netDialTcpDeadline`/
    //     `netReadSockDeadline`/`netWriteSockDeadline`, all `@nosplit` — see
    //     ../../../runtime/net/darwin/tcp.bit's "Deadline-bounded" section). Same
    //     reasoning and same shape as (f4) above: `[]i64(scratchWords(...))` is a
    //     MANAGED local, a real stack-map slot, so `@nosplit` is refused only
    //     because it forbids allocation outright (confirmed by the compiler:
    //     E0075 "may not allocate or call indirectly here", underlining the
    //     slice literal) — not because a raw address survives a poll. The engine
    //     calls below never see the managed local itself, only the packed `*u8`
    //     address computed from it, which is the identical contract every other
    //     ABI wrapper in this file already relies on.
    "net/darwin:netAbiDialDeadlineW",
    "net/darwin:netAbiReadDeadlineW",
    "net/darwin:netAbiWriteDeadlineW",
    "net/linux:netAbiDialDeadlineW",
    "net/linux:netAbiReadDeadlineW",
    "net/linux:netAbiWriteDeadlineW",
  ]
}

// (g) `runtime/net/windows/**` (#3340, #3595) — the IOCP/overlapped-I/O
// socket provider. Same rule (b) as (f) above: every entry here holds no raw
// GC address, only plain OS handles/ints and MANAGED `[]i64` locals (real
// stack-map slots) for any out-parameter or OVERLAPPED/WSABUF scratch. Four
// sub-groups, confirmed against the compiler rather than assumed (`bit check
// runtime/net/windows` after annotating every function this list does NOT
// cover, exit 0, no E0075):
//
//   (g1) `netAwaitReady` — the completion-port readiness probe, this
//   provider's twin of (f1)'s parkers. `fd`/`interest` are plain ints;
//   `taskAddr`/`t`/`wAddr`/`s`/`pollFd` are raw scheduler/kernel addresses,
//   never GC objects. Refused `@nosplit` only because `pollRegister`
//   (../../sched/windows/poll.bit) does not carry the attribute itself.
fn exceptionsNetWindows(): []string {
  return [
    "net/windows:netAwaitReady",
    //   (g2) `netabi.bit` wrappers that forward a caller's `string`/`fd`
    //   straight through to an engine call already in this list, holding no
    //   scratch of their own — the (f3) shape.
    "net/windows:netAbiLocalPort",
    "net/windows:netAbiAccept",
    "net/windows:netAbiDial",
    "net/windows:netAbiWrite",
    "net/windows:netAbiUdpBind",
    "net/windows:netAbiUdpSend",
    //   (g3) `netabi.bit` wrappers that themselves allocate a MANAGED
    //   receive/scratch buffer before or after the engine call — the (f4)/
    //   (f5) shape.
    "net/windows:netAbiRead",
    "net/windows:netAbiUdpRecv",
    "net/windows:netAbiResolve",
    "net/windows:netAbiDialDeadlineW",
    "net/windows:netAbiReadDeadlineW",
    "net/windows:netAbiWriteDeadlineW",
    //   (g4) `sock.bit`'s own managed-scratch helpers for a WSAIoctl/
    //   getsockopt/setsockopt out-parameter. None of the three ever calls
    //   `schedPark` — refused `@nosplit` purely because the attribute
    //   forbids allocation outright (confirmed: E0075 "may not allocate or
    //   call indirectly here" on each `[]i64(...)` literal), not because a
    //   raw address survives anything.
    "net/windows:netSetUpdateAcceptContext",
    "net/windows:netSocketError",
    "net/windows:resolveExtensionFns",
    //   (g4b) `dns.bit`'s `nameserverFromAdapters` (#3589/#3924) -- the same
    //   (g4) shape: refused `@nosplit` purely because it allocates a MANAGED
    //   `[]i64` scratch for `GetAdaptersAddresses`, never because a raw
    //   address survives a poll. See its own header comment in
    //   `runtime/net/windows/dns.bit` for the per-path argument. Its sibling
    //   `firstDnsIpv4`, which walks that buffer via a raw `int`, IS
    //   `@nosplit` -- it needs no exception at all.
    "net/windows:nameserverFromAdapters",
  ]
}

// (g5) `tcp.bit`'s IOCP overlapped-op layer — every OVERLAPPED/WSABUF/
// out-param buffer here is a MANAGED `[]i64` local (see this file's own
// "MANAGED scratch (#1668-style)" comments at each allocation site) that
// must survive the async wait as a real stack-map slot, never a raw address.
// Two independent reasons each is refused `@nosplit`, both tracing to the
// same property: the allocation itself is forbidden outright by E0075, AND
// the call chain reaches `../../sched/windows/poll.bit`'s
// `pollClaimOp`/`pollAssociate`/`pollOp*`, none of which is `@nosplit`.
// `netSockLocalPort` joins this group though it claims no OVERLAPPED: its
// `getsockname` out-length lives in the same MANAGED-local pattern, and darwin/
// linux avoid it only by using a module-static scratch this provider chose
// not to share (see ./sock.bit's own "MANAGED scratch" comments).
fn exceptionsNetTcpEngine(): []string {
  return [
    "net/windows:netSockLocalPort",
    "net/windows:armAndClaim",
    "net/windows:acceptIssue",
    "net/windows:netAcceptTcp",
    "net/windows:dialPrep",
    "net/windows:dialConnect",
    "net/windows:netDialTcp",
    "net/windows:zeroIp",
    "net/windows:netReadSock",
    "net/windows:netWriteSock",
    "net/windows:writeOnce",
    //   (g6) The deadline-bounded wrappers, updated by #3590 -- READ THIS
    //   OVER THE OLD COMMENT, WHICH DESCRIBED A DIFFERENT IMPLEMENTATION.
    //   `netClockNowNs` allocates a MANAGED `[]i64(2)` scratch for
    //   `parkMonoNs`'s out-parameter and never parks — it is a monotonic
    //   clock READ, not a suspend, unchanged since #3595. The three entry
    //   points below and their two new helpers no longer delegate to the
    //   plain unbounded entry point (that was true before #3590; it is not
    //   true now) -- they drive `../../sched/windows/poll.bit`'s
    //   `pollClaimOp`/`pollDrain`/`pollOp*` directly in a bounded loop, none
    //   of which is `@nosplit`, and `dialConnectDeadline`/`writeOnceDeadline`
    //   each allocate the identical MANAGED `[]i64` OVERLAPPED/WSABUF/
    //   out-param locals `dialConnect`/`writeOnce` above already justify by
    //   the same rule.
    "net/windows:netClockNowNs",
    "net/windows:dialConnectDeadline",
    "net/windows:netDialTcpDeadline",
    "net/windows:writeOnceDeadline",
    "net/windows:netReadSockDeadline",
    "net/windows:netWriteSockDeadline",
    //   (g6b) The bounded-wait primitives #3590 added. None allocates: each
    //   holds only plain `int` slot indices/scheduler addresses and a raw
    //   `*byte` into `poll.bit`'s MODULE-STATIC scratch pool (never the GC
    //   heap, never a managed local) — the property rule (b) requires — but
    //   `opPending`/`cancelAndDrain`/`awaitSlotDeadline` still cannot be
    //   `@nosplit` themselves because they call `pollOpOverlapped`/
    //   `pollDrain`, neither of which carries the attribute (same reason
    //   `dialConnect` etc. above are exceptions, not `@nosplit`).
    //   `currentSchedAddr`, by contrast, calls only `schedCurrentTask`/
    //   `taskAt` -- the same two `currentPollFd`/`currentTaskAddr` (#3595)
    //   already call as `@nosplit` -- so it is marked `@nosplit` directly in
    //   tcp.bit rather than listed here.
    "net/windows:opPending",
    "net/windows:cancelAndDrain",
    "net/windows:awaitSlotDeadline",
  ]
}

// (g7) `udp.bit` — `netSetNonBlockUdp` allocates a MANAGED `[]i64(1)` scratch
// for `ioctlsocket`'s FIONBIO argument and never parks; `netBindUdp` calls it.
// `netSendTo`/`netRecvFrom` call `netAwaitReady` (g1) inside their retry
// loop — the identical shape to (f1)'s darwin/linux `netSendTo`/`netRecvFrom`.
fn exceptionsNetUdpEngine(): []string {
  return [
    "net/windows:netSetNonBlockUdp",
    "net/windows:netBindUdp",
    "net/windows:netSendTo",
    "net/windows:netRecvFrom",
  ]
}
